label remembered
[signal arg="bg:support_board"]
The notice went up on the Support Department board a week before it happened.
The Autumn Alchemy Exposition, on the capital's exhibition green, open to the public and to every noble house with an interest in patronage.
Guild scouts would attend.
Lan (Sus): (The Alchemy Exposition.)
Lan (Sus): (In most playthroughs, Florentine walks away with a guild sponsorship, the first real foothold out of the slums.)
Lan (Sus): (In the others, someone sabotages her publicly at the fair, salts her name, and a year and a half later she's back as a very different character.)
Lan (Sus): (The Evil Alchemist. Queen of a poison trade that ran half the continent's underworld.)
Lan (Sus): (I don't know which version of the trigger is coming. I only know I won't find out the hard way.)
Florentine (Flat): Absolutely not.
Florentine (Flat): The last time you got involved in something concerning me, Han's boot was on my satchel and you nearly started a war over spilled mint.
Florentine (Flat): I don't need a bodyguard. I need people to stop assuming I need one.
Lan (Talk): I think something's going to happen at the fair.
Florentine (Flat): Based on what?
Lan (Sus): (Based on two thousand four hundred and fifty hours of a video game you'll never believe exists.)
Lan (Default): A feeling. Call it instinct.
Florentine (Flat): Call it a rank {rank} with a hero complex.
Lan (Default): She'd throw me out on principle. If Veldero's there too, she might let us stay close enough to be useful.
Veldero: You really think something's going to happen?
Lan (Default): I really do.
Veldero: Then I'm in. I'll talk to her. She likes me more than she likes you, which I say with love.
Liesel (Smile): I've read her formulary notes. Her extraction technique on volatile compounds is better than anything they taught us.
Liesel (Smile): I'm not doing this as a favor. I want to watch her work.
Florentine agreed on one condition.
Neither of them hovered, wore anything that looked like armor, or used the word bodyguard within earshot of a paying customer.
label green
[signal arg="bg:exhibition_green"]
The exhibition green sat just outside the capital's western gate, a wide stretch of trimmed grass gone chaotic with striped canopy tents,
guild banners and more foot traffic than Lan had seen since the opening ceremony.
Florentine's booth sat in the third row, modest and slightly undersized.
Labeled vials of burn salve and clotting powder, a shimmering blue antidote base, and, centerpiece,
a tall glass column of something that glowed faintly gold and turned on its own current.
Liesel (Smile): That's beautiful. What's the suspension medium?
Florentine (Flirt): Trade secret. I'll tell you after the judging. Maybe.
Liesel (Smug): I could just test it.
Florentine (Default): You could. I'd notice, and I'd never trust you with anything interesting again.
Veldero: See anything?
Lan (Default): Not yet. I'm sure it's this fair. I don't know the hour. I don't know the shape it takes. Just that if it works, she doesn't get a second fair.
label watch
The afternoon wore on in the grinding tension of watching a crowd for a threat that refused to announce itself.
On his third pass Lan clocked a man.
Middle-aged, well-dressed in the understated way that meant real money, moving with the patience of someone with nowhere else to be.
He'd walked past Florentine's booth twice without buying anything or asking a question.
He paused at the larger booth next door, run by an established guild house, and exchanged a few quiet words with its owner.
Lan (Talk): Veldero.
Veldero: I see him.
They split without discussing it, Veldero drifting toward the man's likely path, Lan angling back toward the booth, close enough to count.
Lan (Sus): (Fourteen vials on the left shelf this morning. Fifteen now.)
label vial
Lan reached the shelf in four unhurried steps,
picked up the extra vial before Florentine finished turning to greet a new customer, and understood the moment it was in his hand.
Small. Unmarked. White, chalky, pressed into a shape he'd last seen skitter across the courtyard flagstones.
Lan (Sus): (Kliam's pills. Here. In her stock.)
Florentine (Mad): That's not mine. I've never seen that vial in my life.
Lan (Default): I know. Someone planted it. Probably counting on a guild scout finding it during the judging walk-through instead of you finding it now.
Florentine (Flat): Framed. For dealing. That would have ended everything.
Across the green, Veldero had closed on the well-dressed man without appearing to hurry.
Lan watched him say something too quiet to carry,
and the man's easy patience cracked for just a second before he turned and walked briskly toward the western gate.
The man was a paid representative of the Voss Guild's flagship stall.
- Pocket the vial quietly
	Lan (Sus): (Nobody saw. If I make a scene, she becomes the story.)
- Pocket it and crack a joke
	set {aff_florentine} += 1
	Lan (Smug): Well. That's one product I won't be sampling.
Lan (Default): It didn't happen. Let's keep it that way.
Liesel (Flat): It didn't work. That's what matters right now.
label ask_veldero
Veldero jogged back a moment later, slightly out of breath.
Veldero: Guild representative. Paid rather than family, I think.
Veldero: He didn't confirm anything, but he didn't deny it very convincingly either,
Veldero: and he left in an enormous hurry for a man who claimed he'd never heard of you.
- Ask what he said to him
	Lan (Sus): What did you say to him?
	Veldero: Nothing clever. I told him I knew where he worked.
- Don't push
	Lan (Default): Good work.
Florentine (Flat): Voss Guild. Of course. They've been undercutting my supplier for two years.
Florentine (Flat): A beastfolk pulling in scout attention at the same fair as their flagship display would have been[pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed]
Liesel (Default): It didn't work. That's what matters right now.
Lan slipped the vial into his own pocket, the small chalky weight of it like a coal that hadn't finished cooling.
Lan (Sus): (One threat, caught in time. Later than I'd like. But caught.)
He didn't yet know how wrong he was about the day being over.
